text:
Let $x = 4$ and $x = \frac{1}{4}$ to get two equations:
\begin{align*}
3f(4) - 2f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) &= 4, \label{eq:1} \\
3f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) - 2f(4) &= \frac{1}{4}. \label{eq:2}
\end{align*}
Subtracting equation \eqref{eq:2} from equation \eqref{eq:1} gives
\begin{align*}
3f(4) - 2f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) - \left(3f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) - 2f(4)\right) &= 4 - \frac{1}{4} \\
5f(4) - 5f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) &= \frac{15}{4}.
\end{align*}
Dividing by 5 gives
\begin{align*}
f(4) - f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) &= \frac{3}{4}.
\end{align*}
Adding this result to equation \eqref{eq:2} gives
\begin{align*}
f(4) + f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right) &= \frac{1}{4} + \frac{3}{4} = 1.
\end{align*}
Adding these two equations eliminates $f\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)$ and gives
\begin{align*}
2f(4) &= 1.
\end{align*}
Therefore, $f(4) = \frac{1}{2}$.

$$\boxed{\frac{1}{2}}$$

